  "tags": ["auth/ownership", "cache invalidation", "persistence", "layered code"],
  "lookFor": [
    "Trace identity: where is req.user set, and what is the property actually called? Follow it into the query.",
    "Mongoose drops `undefined` filter keys, so `{ owner: undefined }` becomes `{}`, which matches EVERYTHING. A leak, not an empty list.",
    "Any write path (create/update/delete) must invalidate every cache the read paths use.",
    "A 200 with the new values does not prove a write. Re-GET after every write to check it stuck.",
    "Check ownership on EVERY endpoint that takes an :id, not just the list."
  ],
  "bugs": [
    {
      "symptom": "My Cars shows other people's cars",
      "file": "src/controllers/carController.js",
      "hints": [
        "Open src/middleware/auth.js. What shape does req.user have?",
        "The controller passes `req.user.userId`. Does that property exist? What does memdb (like Mongoose) do with an undefined filter value?"
      ],
      "cause": "auth sets `req.user = { id }` but the controller reads `req.user.userId` → `undefined`. The filter `{ owner: undefined }` is stripped to `{}` and returns every car in the system. It's an access-control hole, not a display bug.",
      "fix": "Use `req.user.id` everywhere in the controller.",
      "pattern": "Wrong property name → undefined filter → data leak"
    },
    {
      "symptom": "A newly added car never appears",
      "file": "src/services/carService.js",
      "hints": [
        "Was the car never stored, or stored but not returned? Check with GET /cars/:id.",
        "listMine reads from a cache. Who clears that cache?"
      ],
      "cause": "`listMine` caches the list per owner. `updateCar` invalidates it but `addCar` doesn't, so the first list (empty) is served forever.",
      "fix": "Call `invalidate(ownerId)` in `addCar` after the insert.",
      "pattern": "Stale cache: write path doesn't invalidate"
    },
    {
      "symptom": "Edits are lost on refresh",
      "file": "src/services/carService.js",
      "hints": [
        "PATCH returns the new values. Is anything actually written to the DB?",
        "memdb returns COPIES. Mutating the object you fetched changes nothing stored."
      ],
      "cause": "`updateCar` does `Object.assign(car, changes)` on the fetched copy and returns it. Nothing is persisted. The response looks right, so the bug only shows up on the next read.",
      "fix": "`const updated = await carRepo.update(carId, pick(changes, EDITABLE)); invalidate(ownerId); return updated;`",
      "pattern": "Mutating a fetched document without saving"
